#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114987 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "dc6dd9b4-de7f-52d0-8ff9-a7a7f7a9abd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4711" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\n\t\\begin{array}{rl} & x + 5 y + z = 2 \\\\  & x + 5 y + 2 z = 5.\\end{array}\n\t\\right.\n$$\n\n.\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{array}\n\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rlrl}M & \\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & := {R}_{2} + ( - 1 ) {R}_{1} \\\\  & \\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & := {R}_{1} + ( - 1 ) {R}_{2} \\\\  & \\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & 0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{array}$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{rl} & x + 5 y = - 1 \\\\  & z = 3.\\end{array}\n\\right.$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z$, and we have one free variable, $y$. That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y$.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 1 - 5 y \\\\ y = y \\\\ z = 3 ,\\end{array} \\right.$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 - 5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, y \\in  (- \\infty , \\infty )\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114988 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/id #uuid "f2c7cfd3-da43-5208-bd0e-21692357fabe" :question/math-academy-id "e-4712" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 2 y = 1 , \\\\ 4 x + k y = - 3\\end{array} \\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$M=\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & - 3\\end{array}\n\\right].$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rlrl}M & = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & - 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & := {R}_{2} - 2 {R}_{1} \\\\  & \\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k + 4 & - 5\\end{array}\\right]\\end{array}$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{rl} & 2 x - 2 y = 1 \\\\  & ( k + 4 ) y = - 5.\\end{array}\n\\right.$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4$, then the second equation is $0=-5$, which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115041 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/id #uuid "cd56314d-f45a-55be-9762-983fe507a1a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6173" :question/problem "Given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\n\t\\begin{array}{rl} & 2 x - 3 y = 4 \\\\  & - 4 x + 6 y = - 8 ,\\end{array}\n\t\\right.\n$$\n\n,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The system is consistent dependent\n2. The system has no solution\n3. The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 3 & 4 \\\\ - 4 & 6 & - 8\\end{array}\n\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rlrl}M & = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 3 & 4 \\\\ - 4 & 6 & - 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & := {R}_{2} + 2 {R}_{1} \\\\  & \\sim \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & - 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{array}$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\\left\\{\n\t\\begin{array}{rl} & 2 x - 3 y = 4 \\\\  & 0 x + 0 y = 0.\\end{array}\n\t\\right.$$\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation\n\n$$\n0x+0y=0\n$$\n\nwe can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x$, and we have a free variable $y$. That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$.\n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99166388-f058-5f72-b80c-5b4282a522cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113433} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e9df98-67f7-54c0-8ec3-bce6cc775993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = \\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113434} {:answer/id #uuid "f60f9877-fd36-59d9-a3ec-527b98368e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113435} {:answer/id #uuid "284ec6b1-1f86-5100-be3a-3e470bb91463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113436} {:answer/id #uuid "404830dc-4c84-5f9f-b429-3660dc356338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60f9877-fd36-59d9-a3ec-527b98368e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113435} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f40e7bb-c091-5bfa-b08f-acb863997d65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113432}] :question/id #uuid "2a9a3feb-6c16-50e8-885d-ee5cd242ab95" :question/math-academy-id "q-32246" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{rl} & 3 x + 4 y = 12 \\\\  & x + k y = 4\\end{array} \\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a79c34b-32dc-5dd9-a547-05781ee4559b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113830} {:answer/id #uuid "bad97787-15c0-5eb2-8a26-558c015920c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113831} {:answer/id #uuid "be7a233f-6036-5757-ac42-a9794c8286d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113832} {:answer/id #uuid "52522f4f-4d21-50e